## Summary
## 1. Goal
Find violations of Broken Access Control (OWASP A01:2025)¹. Manual review of the source code is done to find potential violations.
## 2. Findings
### 2.1 Insecure user identification
In main.py, more specifically in the functions login, dashboard, upload and download, authentication happens. The id of the authenticated user is then stored inside a cookie. This introduces the potential for a malicious adversary to change the value of the id. As, after authentication, a user is seen as trusted, this could lead to unauthorized access of other users' data.
### 2.2 Path traversal
In filemanager.py (def getFolder, def storeFile), the subject folder is identified by a fix path concatenated with the user id. This id could be modified so that it represents a relative path, leading the two paths concatenated to point to a different location in the file system. This way, access to data could be falsely granted.
## 3. Remediation Plan
### 3.1 Detect tampering
To detect tampering with cookies, one could use locally stored session IDs and check those against the session ID sent by the client.² The session ID itself should be a random string; relevant corresponding data (expiration, user etc.) should be stored server-side.²
### 3.2 Path traversal prevention
To prevent path traversal, following measures could be taken. First, implementing UUIDs (or any random string) instead of integer IDs that simply increment will prevent knowledge of the IDs of other users. Further, sequential periods should be prohibited to prevent users from leaving their intended folder.
